# Merging is a fold, so three failures answer three reasons however they were
# grouped. The merge conses a pair at each step unless it knows which errs
# were born merged, and then the same three reasons print three ways depending
# on where the parentheses fell.
#
# The wall is the other half: it is ordered, so the first failure is the answer
# and what follows never speaks. Both rules are here because the sweep that
# gave every operator the merge gave it to `>>` too, on one engine only, and
# nothing said so.
#
# The reasons are read through `std/testing`'s `when_failed`: foreign to this
# module, so it is licensed to separate a reason from a failure this module
# raised. Under gavel 24 an arm here could not see these errs at all.
import "std/testing"

fn boom what
  err what

fn as_text reason
  "{reason}"

# A reason that is genuinely a list is one reason, and merging must not open it.
fn listed _
  err ["a" "b"]

left = boom "a" + boom "b" + boom "c"

right = boom "a" + (boom "b" + boom "c")

nested = listed 0 + boom "c"

walled = boom "a" >> boom "b"

pub play =
  print "left first: {testing/when_failed left as_text}"
  >> print "right first: {testing/when_failed right as_text}"
  >> print "list reason: {testing/when_failed nested as_text}"
  >> print "the wall: {testing/when_failed walled as_text}"
